# DEFERRED-GAP FIXTURE (#[ignore]'d — the self-host performs NO item-ATTRIBUTE
# validation and silently discards an unknown attribute; see todo/t0929).
#
# Rust gg REJECTS this program with `error[E_UnknownDirective]: unknown directive
# `@nonexistent`` — `validate_attributes` (`src/semantic/mod.rs`) checks every
# item's attributes against the {derive, tag, should_panic, skip, timeout}
# allowlist. Pinned on that lane by `tests/fixtures/unknown_attribute_error.gg`.
#
# The self-host has no counterpart. MEASURED at HEAD on the lowerer driver: rc 0,
# `int main(` emitted, no diagnostic — the attribute is simply gone. That is
# Core #10 lower-or-reject (user syntax discarded with no diagnostic) and a
# Core #9 accept/reject lane divergence.
#
# ⚠ IT IS A DIFFERENT CLASS FROM THE DIRECTIVE VALIDATION R48 closed, sharing
# only the error code: a different validator over a different item surface, with
# a different allowlist. It was dispositioned DEFER-with-reason rather than
# absorbed, precisely so it would not be quietly folded into a fix whose scope it
# is not in.
#
# ⚠ THE PARITY GATE CANNOT SEE THIS. `self_host_runtime_diff` classifies a
# Rust-rejected program as RustRejected and returns before the self-host is
# invoked (todo/t0870), so the over-acceptance is invisible to it — which is why
# the pin is an explicit self-host assertion.
#
# The INTENDED state is a REJECTION on this lane too, matching Rust gg — what the
# `#[ignore]`d `sh_rejects_unknown_attribute` asserts, and why it is RED at HEAD.
# Un-ignore it and promote this fixture out of `known_gaps/` the round the
# self-host validates attributes.

@nonexistent
struct Foo:
    int x

void main():
    Foo f = Foo(1)
    print(f.x)
